b9c87fe0a31b705b3","title":"A canonical scalar can drive accelerated expansion or suppress anisotropy during contraction depending on its pressure relative to its energy density. The Hubble parameter epsilon describes both regimes.","connectionCount":1,"detail":{"shardId":"destination-details-fine-0030"},"indexTemplate":"source_question"},{"id":"source-question:3e128e2c27707a02e0bdeac7f67e0c0dbf99dcf847fc8a5389cab4ad4912c1cc","title":"Can an axion oscillating about a positive minimum produce observable departures from constant dark energy? The proposed low-redshift comparison concerns masses comparable to the present Hubble rate.","connectionCount":1,"detail":{"shardId":"destination-details-fine-0031"},"indexTemplate":"source_question"},{"id":"source-question:3e153bc723c3804a7eeaf56a0a003c852c26f092e81892a6a70c4651a7786b03","title":"The setting is late-time dark energy modeled by a canonical scalar with positive exponential potential on a flat homogeneous and isotropic universe. Its swampland connection is a reported constant-slope attractor identification; the paper does not enforce an explicit quantum-gravity inequality.","connectionCount":1,"detail":{"shardId":"destination-details-fine-0033"},"indexTemplate":"source_question"},{"id":"source-question:3e854198fa1a47b3a773781c5602527768a2d7fcb722fabacd16fb52658344ab","title":"How can monodromy be interpreted in the real five-dimensional M-theory moduli space?","connectionCount":1,"detail":{"shardId":"destination-details-fine-0037"},"indexTemplate":"source_question"},{"id":"source-question:3ec858943221ec2ed1306e5570dd6996b575708450fd9abb06c3233022c0efde","title":"Can the Galactic Center 511 keV annihilation luminosity exclude PBHs as all dark matter in an otherwise viable low-mass window, including extended PBH mass distributions?","connectionCount":1,"detail":{"shardId":"destination-details-fine-0043"},"indexTemplate":"source_question"},{"id":"source-question:3e49df4a3af7e0ef0deacfc8d6c32c231bc24654168a8c4ca8ae9a605